#!/bin/bash
# land-real-shelf.sh — the beauty catalogue becomes seventy real products you
# can actually buy, and the routine grows a body band.
#
# BACKEND ONLY (together-city-chat). Railway, not Vercel.
# RUN THIS BEFORE land-routine-sheet.sh — that page reads fields this adds.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")"
[ -d together-city-chat ] || { echo "!! Run this from the Together-Ai repo root."; exit 1; }

if [ -f .git/index.lock ] && [ ! -s .git/index.lock ]; then
  echo "== clearing a stale empty .git/index.lock"
  rm -f .git/index.lock
fi

NEEDS="A shelf you can buy from"
case "$(git log --oneline -40)" in
  *"$NEEDS"*) echo "== \"$NEEDS\" is already here. Nothing to do."; exit 0 ;;
esac

ALLOWED='^ M together-city-react/(public/assets/img/(apple-touch-icon-180|tc-icon-1024|tc-icon-192|tc-icon-512|tc-icon-maskable-512)\.png|public/downloads/TogetherCity\.apk)$'
DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain \
  | grep -Ev '^\?\? (land-.*\.sh|push-.*\.sh|.*\.patch)$' \
  | grep -Ev "$ALLOWED" || true)"
if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
  echo "!! The tree carries changes this script did not expect:"; echo "$DIRTY"; exit 1
fi

PATCH="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/land.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$PATCH"' EXIT
cat <<'B64EOF' | tr -d '\n' | openssl base64 -d -A > "$PATCH"

B64EOF
WANT="915f899fdd718ac0dac93a80f73f4b5b239023bd793a11e7c995851803a5efe5"
GOT="$(shasum -a 256 "$PATCH" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$GOT" = "$WANT" ] || { echo "!! Patch is corrupt."; echo "   want $WANT"; echo "   got  $GOT"; exit 1; }
echo "== patch verified"
git apply --check -C1 "$PATCH" || { echo "!! Patch does not apply cleanly. Nothing written."; exit 1; }
git apply -C1 "$PATCH"
echo "== applied"

cd together-city-chat
npx tsc --noEmit
npx jest src/beauty
cd ..

git add together-city-chat/src/beauty/beauty-catalog.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/beauty-engine.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/routine-engine.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/catalog-is-shoppable.spec.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/order-pricing.spec.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/routine-engine.spec.ts
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
A shelf you can buy from

The Beauty hub recommended thirteen products from a house brand that does not
exist. They were written to exercise the engine and they did that well — but a
citizen told to use a Ceramide Barrier Cream cannot go and buy a Ceramide
Barrier Cream, and every screen in the hub was ultimately pointing at one.

Seventy real products now, from the owner's data sheet: brand, name, price in
rupees, ingredients, what it is for, two photographs and the page it is sold on.
Minimalist, Cetaphil, CeraVe, The Ordinary, La Roche-Posay, Kama Ayurveda,
Head & Shoulders, Dove. Skincare, hair care and body care.

THE SHELF MOVED TO ITS OWN FILE and beauty-engine re-exports it, because every
reader in the hub has always taken BEAUTY_PRODUCTS from the engine and moving
the data should not move the door.

FIVE FIELDS THE ENGINE NEEDS ARE NOT IN THE SHEET, and this is where the real
work is. `usage`, `suitableSkin`, `profileKeys`, `tags` and the display
`category` are derived from the ingredients, the benefits and the stated skin
type. Every one of those is a judgement, so every one is written down in the
catalogue's header and checked in catalog-is-shoppable.spec.ts — and the next
sheet should be re-run through the same derivation rather than diffed against
this file by hand.

THREE DERIVATIONS THAT WERE WRONG FIRST, which is why the spec exists:

  A RETINOL SERUM WAS SCHEDULED FOR THE MORNING. The rule that puts vitamin C
  in the morning ran after the rule that puts retinoids at night, and a retinol
  serum whose copy also mentions vitamin C came out as a morning product.
  Retinoids increase how easily you burn — the routine has a warning about
  exactly this — and it looked like an ordering detail in a script. The
  retinoid rule runs last now and the spec refuses any retinoid with Morning
  in its usage.

  CAFFEINE PUT `hair-density` ON A FACE MOISTURISER and on a coffee body scrub.
  "UV" in a heat protectant put `spf` on a hair serum. Tags are gated by group
  now, and `spf` only to a sunscreen.

  FOUR PRODUCTS CAME OUT WITH NO `profileKeys` AT ALL. That is not a small
  defect: profileKeys is the PRIMARY match signal, and an empty list scores
  zero for everybody for ever — the product sits on the shelf, unreachable, and
  nothing reports it. The category now supplies a key where the text yields
  none, and the spec fails on an empty list.

`suitableSkin` GATES HARD, so only FACE products are gated at all. A shampoo
listed as suiting oily skin would be withheld from everybody with dry skin,
which is a sentence about the wrong part of their body. "All skin types" in the
sheet's own wording means all; "dry to normal" is a range and expands; three of
the four base types is treated as universal, because at that point the list is
excluding one type by accident.

THE ROUTINE GAINS A BODY BAND. The shelf has fifteen body products and the
routine had three bands, all of them face and hair. Body care is its own band
rather than a fourth step in a considered face routine — nothing in the skin and
hair assessment has an opinion about your elbows — but it is on the shelf, so it
is listed somewhere it can be followed.

AND HAIR GETS ITS OWN ORDER. One `/haircare/` rule gave every hair product the
same rank, so a conditioner could be printed above the shampoo it has to follow.
Oil, wash, condition, treat, finish — five ranks, and a test that shuffles the
input and expects that order out.

"Weekly" is now "Weekly & wash day": shampoo, conditioner and a pre-wash oil
live in that band, and the old title made a wash day read as an optional extra.
The step frequency moved to a table for the same reason — `when === 'weekly' ?
'Once a week'` stopped being true the moment shampoo arrived.

TWO PHOTOGRAPHS PER PRODUCT, from different retailers. A hotlinked image is the
one field here guaranteed to rot, and a shelf of broken frames reads as a broken
shop rather than a missing picture. The screens try the first, fall to the
second, and end at a category mark.

order-pricing.spec.ts took its two products from the shelf by name; both were
invented and both left. It takes the first two products off the front of the
catalogue now, because pricing does not care which product it is — it cares
that the price comes from the shelf and not from the request body.

tsc clean, the beauty jest suite green — seven spec files, 65 tests, including
an end-to-end one that builds a person with four real readings a routine and
requires steps in all four bands.

MSG
echo "== committed"

echo
echo "==============================================================="
echo " Landed: $NEEDS"
echo " Seventy real products, four routine bands. Railway."
echo " Run land-routine-sheet.sh next."
echo "==============================================================="
